class_name DirectorCodepage
extends RefCounted
## Which single-byte codepage a title's text is in, both ways.
##
## Director stored every string -- member names, marker names, field text, Lingo
## source -- as **bytes in the authoring machine's script system**, one byte per
## character. There is no encoding declaration anywhere in the container: the
## codepage is a fact about the title, not about the file, which is why it is
## configured rather than sniffed.
##
## Until this existed the port did neither half. `director_cast.gd` read with
## `get_string_from_ascii`, which maps byte *n* to code point *n* -- a Latin-1
## pass-through -- and `director_writer.gd` wrote the exact inverse, one byte per
## character with **anything at or above 256 written as `0x3f`**. That pairing is
## lossless for text that came out of a container, which is why nothing broke for
## as long as nothing new was typed. A real keyboard is the case it cannot
## survive: Hebrew alef is U+05D0, so every Hebrew character a player typed into
## a save name was destroyed on the way to disc and the slot came back as
## `?????`. `games/piposh2/HEZSAVE.DIR` carries exactly that -- `gamename1` is
## five bytes of `0x3f` -- from a save made before this landed.
##
## **The codepage is measured, not assumed.** This corpus is Mac-authored (its
## `ccl` paths say `Macintosh HD:`) but was mass-converted through Director on
## Windows, so Mac OS Hebrew and Windows-1255 were both live candidates. They
## agree on the letters -- both put alef..tav at 0xE0..0xFA -- so the letters
## cannot tell them apart, and every discriminating byte is elsewhere:
##
## | evidence | Mac OS Hebrew | Windows-1255 |
## |---|---|---|
## | `<FA><EE><E5><F0><E4><A0>1`, a bitmap's name in 22 members | `tmwnh 1` | `tmwnh<NBSP>1` |
## | `cardfield<B2>`, a field member's name | `cardfield2` | `cardfield` + superscript 2 |
## | member names that are only `<B3>`, `<B4>`, `<B5>` | `3`, `4`, `5` | superscript 3, acute, micro |
## | `<B9>` inside `searchinfo`, a comma-separated numeric column | `9` | superscript 1 |
## | `<A1><A0><A1><A0><A1>`, a cast member's name | `! ! !` | three inverted `!` |
## | bytes 0x8A 0x8C 0x8D 0x8E 0x8F 0x9C 0x9D 0xD6, all present | accented Latin | **undefined** |
##
## Every row says Mac OS Hebrew, and the last says it on its own: those eight
## bytes have no meaning at all in Windows-1255, so no file containing them was
## written in it. Decoded as Mac OS Hebrew, `piposh/ROOT/strtgame`'s
## `BehaviorScript 405` -- an `alert(...)` -- reads as a Hebrew sentence through a
## second layer of 1997 mojibake ("the game must be loaded from the main menu
## only"); decoded as Windows-1255 it is undefined bytes.
## `tools/text_codepage.gd` is that measurement, as a gate.
##
## **Mac script systems are not one-to-one, and the encoder resolves it.** Mac OS
## Hebrew carries a second copy of most of ASCII at 0xA0-0xBF and 0xFB-0xFF --
## 0xA0 is a space, 0xB0-0xB9 the digits, 0xBF a question mark -- because a
## right-to-left run needed its own copies. Decoding is unambiguous; encoding has
## to choose, and it chooses the **lowest byte** that produces the character, so
## a space goes back as 0x20 and a `?` as 0x3f. A member whose authored name used
## 0xB2 re-encodes to 0x32: the same text, different bytes.
##
## Over all six titles that is 71 authored strings, and the writer can only ever
## reach the 3 of them that are field members -- `searchinfo` in AIR1, DAY1 and
## NIGHT1, where a `0xB9` is a `9` inside a comma-separated numeric column. No
## script in any title writes `searchinfo`, so no save touches it; and if one
## ever did, the field would come back with the same nine and one byte fewer of
## 1997. The invariant that actually matters is that the **text** is a fixed
## point, and `tools/text_codepage.gd` asserts that over all 47,000 authored
## strings in `games/` rather than trusting this paragraph.
##
## Title-agnostic. No table here knows what game is loaded; which one is in force
## is answered by `active()` from the configuration and the command line.


## The name that means "do what this port has always done": byte *n* becomes code
## point *n*, and back again. It is not a Director codepage and is not a guess at
## one -- it is the identity, and it is the default so that a title nobody has
## measured keeps the behaviour it had rather than acquiring a wrong one.
const IDENTITY := "latin1"

## `[game] codepage` is read from here when nothing on the command line overrides
## it. The same file `director_paths.gd` reads the root from, and for the same
## reason: one per-title settings file, not two.
const CONFIG_PATH := "res://director_game.cfg"

## Code point for each byte 0x80..0xFF. Bytes 0x00..0x7F are ASCII in every one
## of these and are never table-driven.
##
## Generated from the Unicode Consortium's vendor mappings -- Python's own `mac_*`
## and `cp*` codecs for eight of them, Apple's `HEBREW.TXT` for the ninth, which
## no runtime ships. Three Mac OS Hebrew bytes decompose to more than one code
## point (0x81, 0xC0, 0xDE); each is taken as the first of its sequence, which
## keeps the table one-to-one. None of the three occurs anywhere in `games/`.
